//! The connector catalog: MCP servers a deployment may be wired to.
//!
//! # Why a catalog and not a URL field
//!
//! `ctx.egress(name)` is scoped to the destinations declared in `ferrite.json`,
//! and the platform refuses a wildcard outright
//! (`APP_EGRESS_DESTINATION_MALFORMED` — this repository already hit that when a
//! link-preview handle tried `https://`). An arbitrary MCP URL supplied at
//! runtime therefore **cannot be dialled**. That is the platform's SSRF defence,
//! and it is not negotiable.
//!
//! So a connector's HOST is a deploy-time decision and its AUTHORIZATION is a
//! runtime one:
//!
//! | What | When |
//! | --- | --- |
//! | the host is declared in the app spec | deploy time |
//! | an operator enables the connector | runtime |
//! | a user authenticates it (the button) | runtime, per user |
//! | its tools appear in an agent's surface | runtime, after auth |
//! | a NEW provider nobody declared | spec edit + redeploy |
//!
//! # Provenance is a field, not a footnote
//!
//! Checked against the providers' own documentation (2026-08):
//!
//! * **Cloudflare** documents fifteen remote servers, streamable-HTTP on `/mcp`
//!   with a deprecated SSE path on `/sse`.
//! * **Linear** documents `mcp.linear.app/mcp`, explicitly OAuth 2.1 with
//!   dynamic client registration.
//! * **GitHub** does not document a GitHub-operated remote MCP endpoint. A
//!   community list names `api.githubcopilot.com/mcp`; that is recorded as
//!   [`Provenance::Community`] so an operator enabling it knows what it rests on.
//! * **Figma** publishes a **local** dev-mode server on `127.0.0.1:3845`. A
//!   deployed app cannot reach a client's loopback on any host it could declare,
//!   so this is not a scoping limitation to lift — see [`UNAVAILABLE`].
//! * **Vercel / v0** publishes no remote MCP endpoint.
//!
//! Recording the last three rather than omitting them is the point: "where is
//! Figma?" should have an answer in the code.
//!
//! # Why the catalog is spread across SEVERAL handles
//!
//! A capability token may carry at most sixteen key prefixes
//! (the platform's `MAX_PREFIXES`), and an egress declaration's
//! destinations become those prefixes **verbatim** when the platform mints the
//! egress capability. So
//! [`MAX_DESTINATIONS_PER_HANDLE`] is a hard platform bound on how many
//! connectors ONE handle can reach, and this catalog has more connectors than
//! that.
//!
//! Measured, not inferred: declaring all seventeen under one handle made the
//! deploy fail with `malformed capability: too many key prefixes (max 16)` — not
//! at spec validation, but after the build, so it refused the whole app rather
//! than the one grant.
//!
//! Each entry therefore names the handle it rides, and the spec declares one
//! grant per handle. Grouping is by PROVIDER because that is what an operator
//! reasons about: "this deployment may reach Cloudflare" is a decision, whereas
//! "this deployment may reach connectors 1–15" is an artefact of a limit.

/// How well established an endpoint is.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Provenance {
    /// The provider's own documentation names this endpoint.
    Documented,
    /// A community list names it and the provider does not document it. Usable,
    /// but an operator should know it is unverified.
    Community,
}

/// The platform's cap on how many destinations ONE egress handle may name.
